<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      S1-09. `ops.health.view` — may this person see whether the SOFTWARE is running.

      ══ What the screen is ══

      /app/settings/health lists every service the gateway routes to, whether it answered its
      health probe in the last few seconds, and when it was last reachable. It contains no
      restaurant data of any kind: no orders, no money, no customers, no staff. It answers exactly
      one question, and it is a question the product previously had no way to ask —
      "is it broken, or is it me?"

      The 2026-08-12 register recorded a manager getting `503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE` on the till, the
      kitchen board, the customer file and payroll simultaneously, and noted that "the product's
      only explanation is a generic error". There was no health route anywhere under
      frontend/app/(tenant)/app/ — the directory held crm, dashboard, finance, hr, inventory,
      kitchen, menu, nlq, pos, profile, purchasing, reports, settings, stations, tables, terminals
      and users, and nothing else. An operator's only recourse was to ask a developer to run `ps`.

      ══ Who holds it, and why not everyone ══

      OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN. These are the two people who telephone support, and /app/settings is
      already their surface — a branch MANAGER is refused there today (measured live: "Access
      denied" on /app/settings as manager@terrace.local), so putting the screen under settings and
      granting it to settings' own holders keeps one story rather than inventing a second.

      A cashier is NOT granted it, and that is a scoping decision rather than an information one:
      the POS terminal now tells whoever is standing at it, in plain language, that the ordering
      service is unreachable and what still works. The person at the till learns what they need
      from the till. The fleet-wide list — every service, its route prefixes, its instance count —
      is an administrator's view of the deployment, and there is no reason to put the shape of the
      estate in front of everyone who can ring up a coffee.

      Not MANAGER either, for now. If it turns out the branch manager is the one who needs this at
      8pm on a Saturday, the fix is another explicit grant here, argued on its own — not a widening
      of what the code means.

      ══ Why the grant is explicit ══

      Same reason 055, 059, 082, 083, 085 and 088 each give: every blanket "grant the whole
      catalogue to OWNER" changeset in this changelog is point-in-time and retro-grants nothing
      added after it ran. A permission that no role holds does not fail at startup, does not break
      a test, and produces a clean confident 403 for every user including OWNER — which is
      indistinguishable from a deliberate denial. That is the exact defect class
      PermissionCatalogClosureTest exists to catch, and it is why the verification changeSet below
      RAISEs rather than warns.
    -->

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-089-ops-health-view" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>ops.health.view — read the operator service-health screen; held by OWNER and TENANT_ADMIN</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
            INSERT INTO permissions (code, module, description) VALUES
              ('ops.health.view', 'ops', 'View which services are running and when each was last reachable (no restaurant data)')
            ON CONFLICT (code) DO NOTHING;

            INSERT INTO role_permissions (role_code, permission_code) VALUES
              ('OWNER',        'ops.health.view'),
              ('TENANT_ADMIN', 'ops.health.view')
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" endDelimiter=";">
                DELETE FROM role_permissions WHERE permission_code = 'ops.health.view';
                DELETE FROM permissions WHERE code = 'ops.health.view';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="auth-1.0.0-089-verify-ops-health-view" author="restaurantos-agent" runOnChange="false">
        <comment>Fail the migration if ops.health.view exists but no role holds it</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false">
            DO $$
            DECLARE holder_count INT;
            BEGIN
                SELECT count(*) INTO holder_count
                  FROM role_permissions
                 WHERE permission_code = 'ops.health.view';
                IF holder_count = 0 THEN
                    RAISE EXCEPTION
                      'ops.health.view is defined but granted to no role. The gateway''s FleetHealthController refuses GET /api/v1/ops/health without it, so the operator health screen would render an access refusal for every user on every tenant including OWNER - and it would do so as an ordinary-looking permission denial, not an error anyone would trace back to this migration.';
                END IF;
            END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
